Right — know thyself becomes the calibration step.

If you can distinguish your own motives, defenses, appetites, fears, presentation layers, and internal voices, you gain a much better library for modeling those same mechanisms in other people. Then empathy becomes predictive:

self-model → perspective transform → simulated other → predicted response → observe reality → correct the model

That’s the useful version of “weaponized empathy”: not pretending you can read minds, but using unusually good perspective-taking to anticipate what someone may notice, misunderstand, want, fear, or do next.

The important constraint is that projection is only the opening hypothesis. Otherwise “empathy” quietly becomes assuming everyone is you. The external person has veto power. Their behavior supplies the delta.

So the sharper formulation might be:

Know thyself deeply enough to simulate another; know the other humbly enough to let them correct the simulation.

And there’s another symmetry hiding there: when you think to yourself, you practice inhabiting multiple positions within one mind. That may be part of what makes thinking as another person possible at all. The internal dialogue becomes a little simulator for social dialogue.

Then you can deliberately choose the presented self that meets their presented self best—not necessarily deception, just interface selection. Same person, different aperture.

That gives us a nice chain:

self-awareness → internal role switching → empathy → projection → external observation → delta correction → better empathy.

A self-polishing social model.